.TH "MAW-MODULES" "5" "" "maw 0.2.1" "maw manual"
.SH NAME
maw-modules \- writing modules: lib.program, lib.raw, config.nix
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
A dotfiles repo looks like this:
.PP
.EX
dotfiles/
  default.nix    # import <maw> { dotfiles = ./.; }
  config.nix     # your settings: fonts, colors, anything modules share
  maw.nix        # written by maw: packages, services, recorded paths
  modules/       # one <name>.nix per program
.EE
.SH "A MODULE"
.PP
A module is a function that returns the files for one program. It names the program, never a path. maw works out where the file goes.
.PP
.EX
{ config, lib, ... }:
lib.program "foot" {
  format = "ini";
  settings = {
    main.font = "${config.font.mono}:size=${toString config.font.size}";
    colors = config.colors.foot;
  };
}
.EE
.PP
The arguments:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBconfig\fR: the contents of \fBconfig.nix\fR, with this machine's \fBhosts/<name>.nix\fR merged over it. It can be an attrset, or a function taking any of \fB{ lib, theme, host }\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlib\fR: the nixpkgs lib plus maw's additions (below).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmaw\fR: the contents of \fBmaw.nix\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtheme\fR: the palette from the current wallpaper, \fB{ }\fR before there is one; see "Wallpaper themes" in \fBmaw help usage\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBhost\fR: this machine's name; see "More than one machine" in \fBmaw help usage\fR. \fBlib.onHosts [ "laptop" ] <files>\fR gives the files on those machines and nothing elsewhere.
.PP
A module takes the arguments it names; list only the ones you use.
.SH "LIB.PROGRAM NAME { ... }"
.PP
.EX
option      default               meaning
format      "raw"                 how to render settings; see maw help formats
settings                          the file's contents as Nix values
files       { main = settings; }  several files, keyed by role
executable  false                 mark the output executable
path                              a subpath for the main file, when the program needs one
scope       "user"                "user" or "root"
reload      the registry's        a command run after the files change, so the running program reads them
.EE
.PP
Programs with more than one file use \fBfiles\fR. \fBformat\fR can then be one string for all files, or an attrset with one format per file:
.PP
.EX
lib.program "waybar" {
  format = { main = "json"; style = "css"; };
  files = {
    main = { layer = "top"; modules-right = [ "clock" ]; };
    style."window#waybar".background = "#1e1e2e";
  };
}
.EE
.PP
A plain string or \fBlib.raw\fR as a file's settings is written verbatim, whatever the format.
.SH "LIB.SERVICE NAME { ... }"
.PP
Describes a runit service. maw writes its \fBrun\fR and \fBlog/run\fR files and enables it; see "Services" in \fBmaw help usage\fR.
.PP
.EX
option  default   meaning
scope   "system"  "system" runs from boot as root, "user" runs as you from login
run               the script: shell text, or a whole script with its own #! line
log     true      log through svlogd to /var/log/<name>/ or ~/.local/state/log/<name>/
enable  true      link it into place; false writes the definition but leaves it off
env     { }       variables exported before run, literally: no $ expansion
.EE
.PP
.EX
{ config, lib, ... }:
lib.service "rclone" {
  scope = "user";
  env.RCLONE_VFS_CACHE_MODE = "full";
  run = ''
    exec rclone --config "$HOME/.config/rclone/rclone.conf" mount "Google Drive:" "$HOME/Google Drive"
  '';
}
.EE
.PP
The run script gets \fBexec 2>&1\fR first, so errors reach the log, then the \fBenv\fR exports. It should end by \fBexec\fRing the long-running program, which runit supervises and restarts when it exits. A module can return a service alongside program files as a list: \fB[ (lib.program "x" { ... }) (lib.service "x" { ... }) ]\fR.
.SH "RELOADING"
.PP
When an activation changes a program's files, maw runs its reload command once all files are in place: \fBpkill -USR2 -x waybar\fR, \fBdunstctl reload\fR, \fBmakoctl reload\fR, and so on. The registry knows the command for common programs; \fBreload\fR in \fBlib.program\fR sets or overrides it:
.PP
.EX
lib.program "waybar" {
  reload = "pkill -USR2 -x waybar";
  ...
}
.EE
.PP
The command runs through \fBsh -c\fR as you. A program that isn't running fails its reload, which is fine. Programs that watch their own config, like niri and alacritty, need none. Services restart on their own instead; see \fBlib.service\fR.
.SH "LIB.DCONF { ... }"
.PP
Desktop settings: the dconf database that GTK apps, GNOME, and \fBgsettings\fR read. Keys are grouped by path:
.PP
.EX
{ lib, ... }:
lib.dconf {
  "org/gnome/desktop/interface" = {
    color-scheme = "prefer-dark";
    gtk-theme = "Adwaita-dark";
    font-name = "Adwaita Sans 11";
    text-scaling-factor = 1.25;
  };
}
.EE
.PP
Strings, numbers, booleans, and lists become GVariant values; for anything else (\fBuint32 5\fR, tuples), write the GVariant text with \fBlib.raw\fR. A module can return settings next to program files as a list: \fB[ (lib.program "x" { ... }) (lib.dconf { ... }) ]\fR. Two modules setting one key to different values is an error.
.PP
maw writes each key with \fBdconf write\fR, and only when its value differs. A key you change by hand afterwards, say in a settings app, is reported and left alone, like a hand-edited file; \fBmaw activate --force\fR puts the declared value back. Keys you stop declaring are reset to their defaults, unless you've changed them since. dconf needs a desktop session: activating from a bare console skips settings with a note, and the next activation in your session applies them.
.SH "VERBATIM TEXT"
.PP
\fBlib.raw ''...''\fR is accepted anywhere a value is. Use it where Nix syntax doesn't reach, and mix it freely with Nix in one file:
.PP
.EX
settings = {
  main.pad = "8x8";
  bindings = lib.raw ''
    [key-bindings]
    clipboard-copy=Control+Shift+c
  '';
};
.EE
.SH "ORDERING"
.PP
Nix sorts attrset keys alphabetically, so rendered files come out sorted. When order matters, like niri window rules or CSS cascade, use a list.
.SH "SHARING VALUES"
.PP
Anything several modules use, like colors, fonts, or your terminal, belongs in \fBconfig.nix\fR:
.PP
.EX
{
  font.ui = "Adwaita Sans";
  colors.text = "f2f2f7";
  terminal = "ghostty";
}
.EE
.PP
Then \fBconfig.colors.text\fR in fuzzel, waybar, and niri all read the same value. With a \fBmaw help usage\fR, colors can come from the palette instead, in one place:
.PP
.EX
{ lib, theme }:
{
  colors.text = lib.removePrefix "#" theme.colors.on_surface;
  colors.accent = lib.removePrefix "#" theme.colors.primary;
}
.EE
.PP
 Plain \fBlet\fR bindings and functions work too, for repetition inside one module:
.PP
.EX
let
  workspaceBinds = prefix: action:
    lib.listToAttrs (map (n: lib.nameValuePair "${prefix}${toString n}" { ${action} = n; }) (lib.range 1 9));
in
# ...
binds = workspaceBinds "Mod+" "focus-workspace" // { "Mod+Q".close-window = null; };
.EE
.PP
\fBtests/fixtures/dotfiles/modules/\fR has complete real-world modules for niri, waybar, and fuzzel.
.SH "RENDERING A MODULE BY HAND"
.PP
\fB-I maw=\fR points at maw's \fBnix/\fR directory:
.PP
.EX
nix-instantiate --eval --strict --raw -I maw=./nix \e
  -E '(builtins.head (import ./path/to/dotfiles).modules.fuzzel).content'
.EE
.PP
Each module evaluates to a list of \fB{ name, key, content, executable, scope }\fR, one entry per file.
